using System.Text.Json;

namespace {{ .name }};

/// <summary>
/// How this project's contract types are read and written.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// The generated types carry their serialization settings in a class this
/// project cannot expose directly — the generator emits it as
/// <c>internal</c>. This file is the seam: it lives in the same assembly, so
/// it can see those settings, and it is the only thing a component or the
/// framework depends on.
///
/// That indirection is the point. Swapping the generator changes this file
/// and <c>generate-contracts.ps1</c>, and nothing else: a generator that
/// emits attribute-only types with no settings object of its own would return
/// plain options here and every caller would keep working.
/// </remarks>
public static class ContractOptions
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The options every component uses to deserialize a message payload, and
    /// the options the framework encodes one with when a component registers
    /// them. Reading a schema's string enums back as the schema spells them —
    /// hyphens included — depends on the converters these carry.
    /// </summary>
    public static JsonSerializerOptions Options => Converter.Settings;
}
